{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1271 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "e67c4cbf9d1a09153521ae414fcfe7404ed95c41bc934f58c14ba21a920868e8" :edb/db-before-t 1270 :edb/db-after-t 1271 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534583 50 #inst "2026-10-12T03:47:28.027Z" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186192505 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under scalar multiplication. For example, the vector ![](images/6d/6da77166b57246e3d4bb076af75c54a10350678986adfb6e31125e0ef614bacc.svg) belongs to $A,$ but the vector ![](images/9c/9cfd2972828a5c45bc58b434debbcf4a96c2cab14daa36a1e07fc37011682960.svg) does not lie in $A$ (because it does not satisfy $x^2=y$).\n\n- The set $B$ represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have\n![](images/83/83ecb68263c21fca495034a2f1678dced07ddf9f5167c5a0a9c7703657530c47.svg)\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3-t=0 \\\\ -3+t=0 \\\\ -6+2t=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $t=3$ is the solution to the three equations. So, if we choose $t=3,$ then we find that $\\mathbf{0} \\in B.$ Therefore, $B$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186192505 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under scalar multiplication. For example, the vector ![](images/6d/6da77166b57246e3d4bb076af75c54a10350678986adfb6e31125e0ef614bacc.svg) belongs to $A,$ but the vector ![](images/9c/9cfd2972828a5c45bc58b434debbcf4a96c2cab14daa36a1e07fc37011682960.svg) does not lie in $A$ (because it does not satisfy $x^2=y$).\n\n- The set $B$ represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have\n![](images/83/83ecb68263c21fca495034a2f1678dced07ddf9f5167c5a0a9c7703657530c47.svg)\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3-t=0 \\\\ -3+t=0 \\\\ -6+2t=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $t=3$ is the solution to the three equations. So, if we choose $t=3,$ then we find that $\\mathbf{0} \\in B.$ Therefore, $B$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." 13194139534583 17592186045417 false] [17592186192780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426532 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186192781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426533 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186192781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426535 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186192782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426531 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186192782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426534 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186192782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426536 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186247159 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x-4y=1}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x=-2y}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0}$" 13194139534583 17592186045417 false] [17592186247159 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x-4y=1\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x=-2y\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0\\}$" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186317380 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0}$\n- $B={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=2y}$\n- $C={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0}$" 13194139534583 17592186045417 false] [17592186317380 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=2y\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0\\}$" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186342191 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}<\\frac{1}{2}}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}: