139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426532 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426528 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426533 1069 "q-110763" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426533 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨1+t,t-1⟩\\,:\\,t\\in R\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:xy=0\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=0\\}$" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426533 1072 #uuid "784a3447-3ec5-5b3c-aa05-c4909c41863a" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426533 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+t \\\\ t-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}t+1=0 \\\\ t-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the system has no solution. So, $0$ is not in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace. Indeed, both $a=⟨1,0⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1⟩$ belong to $B$ but \n\n$$\na+b=⟨1,0⟩+⟨0,1⟩=⟨1,1⟩\n$$\n\n \ndoes not satisfy the condition $xy=0$ since $1\\cdot 1\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It represents a line (the $y$-axis), and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=0$ since $0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426526 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426534 1069 "q-158073" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426534 1071 "![](images/f6/f6695911ed29ccf88240660e45ce6527c0a10e3cf8d6a299ac9eef2be3ff53cb.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426534 1072 #uuid "8d538e48-6d40-5139-9e4a-4a2045c240b7" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426534 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426525 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426535 1069 "q-110759" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426535 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x-y+z=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=0\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨0,0,1⟩\\}$" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426535 1072 #uuid "c109cc3b-b729-5d92-be15-5107dfc714ce" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426535 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y+z=0$ since $0-0+0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. This subset has only one element, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩,$ which is a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. This subset has only one element, the vector $⟨0,0,1⟩,$ so it does not contain the zero vector.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426527 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426536 1069 "q-110599" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426536 1071 "![](images/c3/c36c34462f2ba7f129ba3370d4e67c5ac450b4188be629a256caf7807d4cb2d6.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426536 1072 #uuid "d4fcfc53-7279-50f5-90f1-bd8c9662d8f3" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true] [17592186426536 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534583 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0767be10-b80e-5b7a-9933-3ba3fb4c44e8" 17592186426495 "answer-0d497d71-015c-5091-8ed9-1e92e99927de" 